    "q-156048": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156048",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2y-8}{3}\\le \\frac{8-2y}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{2y-8}{3}) & \\le 12\\cdot (\\frac{8-2y}{4}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
