    "q-156062": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156062",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2a-4}{8}\\le \\frac{2a}{6}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a-4}{8}) & \\le 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a}{6}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-156062-a",
              "type": "math",
